A sequence $(x_n)$ of rational numbers is:
- \emph{bounded above} if $\exists \Lambda \in \mathbb{Q}\; \forall n \in \mathbb{N},\;
 x_n \leq \Lambda$;
- \emph{bounded below} if $\exists \lambda \in \mathbb{Q}\; \forall n \in \mathbb{N},\;
 x_n \geq \lambda$;
- \emph{bounded} if $\exists \Gamma \in \mathbb{Q},\;\Gamma>0\;\;
 \forall n \in \mathbb{N},\; |x_n| \leq \Gamma$.

nodes.statement_tex:
A sequence $(x_n)$ of rational numbers is:
\begin{itemize}
 \item \emph{bounded above} if $\exists \Lambda \in \mathbb{Q}\; \forall n \in \mathbb{N},\;
 x_n \leq \Lambda$;
 \item \emph{bounded below} if $\exists \lambda \in \mathbb{Q}\; \forall n \in \mathbb{N},\;
 x_n \geq \lambda$;
 \item \emph{bounded} if $\exists \Gamma \in \mathbb{Q},\;\Gamma>0\;\;
 \forall n \in \mathbb{N},\; |x_n| \leq \Gamma$.
\end{itemize}

nodes.source_text:
This theorem says that the Stern--Brocot tree does not merely enumerate
the rational numbers.  Its infinite paths encode all positive irrational
numbers as limits of rational approximations.

Thus:

\begin{itemize}
\item finite Stern--Brocot paths correspond to rational numbers;
\item infinite Stern--Brocot paths correspond to irrational numbers.
\end{itemize}

In this way the Stern--Brocot tree reaches beyond $\mathbb{Q}$ and
touches the full continuum of positive real numbers.

nodes.statement_display:
Let $f : A \to \mathbb{R}$ and let $c$ be a cluster point of $A$.
Then $\lim_{x\to c}f(x)=L$ exists for some
$L \in \mathbb{R}$ if and only if
\[
 \ForallOT{\varepsilon},\;
 \ExistsIT{\delta},\;
 \forall x, y \in A :\;
 0<|x-c|<\delta
 \;\wedge\;
 0<|y-c|<\delta
 \;\Rightarrow\;
 |f(x)-f(y)|<\varepsilon.
\]

